    - name: "Inspect Required Final Score"
      text: "Instantly view your current weighted average, letter grade, and the required final exam score."
faqs:
  - q: "How is a weighted grade calculated?"
    a: "To calculate a weighted grade, multiply each assignment score by its percentage weight, add all the resulting points together, and divide by the total completed weight percentage. Mathematically: Weighted Grade = Σ(Score × Weight) ÷ Σ(Weight)."
  - q: "How do you calculate what score you need on the final exam?"
    a: "Subtract your current weighted points contribution from your total required points, then divide by the final exam's weight percentage: Required Score = (Target Points - Current Points) ÷ Final Weight. For example, if you need 30 points and the final is worth 30% of the class, you need 30 ÷ 0.30 = 100% on the final."
  - q: "What happens if total assignment weights do not add up to 100%?"
    a: "Our calculator automatically divides your accumulated points by the sum of completed weights. This normalizes your score to accurately represent your true current standing at any point in the semester."
  - q: "What should I do if my syllabus uses total points instead of percentages?"
    a: "If your syllabus uses points (e.g. Homework 200 pts, Midterm 300 pts, Final 500 pts out of 1000), divide each category's points by the total course points to find the percentage weight: 200 ÷ 1000 = 20% weight."
  - q: "What does it mean if the calculator says I need over 100% on the final?"
    a: "If the required score exceeds 100%, it means mathematical perfection on the final exam alone will fall short of your target grade. You would need extra credit points or a curved grading scale to reach that target."
  - q: "What does it mean if the calculator says I need 0% or negative on the final?"
    a: "This means you have already accumulated enough weighted points throughout the term that you are mathematically guaranteed to achieve your target grade, even with a zero on the final test."
  - q: "Does this grade calculator save my private coursework grades?"
    a: "No. All grade arithmetic runs purely client-side in your local browser window. No assignment names, test scores, or personal academic records are transmitted or saved."
