      <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Grade Calculation Pitfalls</h2>
      <ul style="color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.8; margin-left: 1.25rem;">
        <li><strong>Simple Averaging Instead of Weighting:</strong> Taking a simple mathematical average of test scores ignores that a 20-minute quiz and a comprehensive midterm exam carry vastly different weight percentages in your syllabus.</li>
        <li><strong>Failing to Normalize Incomplete Semesters:</strong> If only 50% of coursework is completed, having 42 total accumulated points does not mean you have a 42% (F). You must divide by 0.50, revealing an 84% (B).</li>
        <li><strong>Assuming 100% Weight Sum Before Finals:</strong> Your completed assignments will rarely sum to 100% until the final exam is graded. Always account for remaining final exam weight when calculating current standing.</li>
      </ul>
